            <div xml:id="d174">

                <pb facs="0335" n="221" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg221"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/4062: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Garrett</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-06-23T20:30:00+01:00">June 23, 1931—8:30
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 23—6:37 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>105. Following is translation of telegram which Chief of Government<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d174-f1"/> sent this evening to Italian Ambassador at Washington and
                        <pb facs="0336" n="222" xml:id="pg222"/> of which Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs has just handed me original text: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I beg Tour Excellency to inform the Government of the United States that
                            I have examined the proposal of President Hoover for the total
                            suspension during a year of the payment of the debts between
                            governments.</p>
                        <p>This proposal necessitates considerable sacrifices on the part of Italy
                            but I, after mature reflection, have decided to give it my cordial
                            acceptance in principle. I expect to convey later to the American
                            Government some observations on my part concerning an application at
                            once equitable and practical, as is certainly within the intention of
                            President Hoover, of the happy initiative of the American Government.
                            Hope that the initiative of President Hoover of which the high moral
                            significance has been perfectly understood by the Italian people, will
                            inaugurate a period of the most successful collaboration between the
                            nations so much more necessary in this moment of general difficulties
                            and on the eve of the Disarmament Conference.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>The Minister for Foreign Affairs informs me that it is proposed to give the
                    foregoing to the press tomorrow at noon.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Garrett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
